
# Week 4 – Conversion of MRI Images to Binary Using Python

**Project:** Brain MRI Tumor Segmentation and Quantitative Analysis Using Classical Image Processing  
**Course:** ECE501 - Digital Image Processing  
**Group:** G9 - The Medics

## Team
- Kush Kelaiya — AU2440018
- Rushin Modi — AU2440028
- Kritee Shah — AU2440080
- Jash Odedara — AU2440096

## Work Completed
- Converted the entire MRI image series into binary format for more efficient processing.
- Explored different filtering techniques using concepts learned in the course.
- Identified MRI scans without visible tumors and used them as baseline images for comparison with other scans.
- Converted image data from `float64` to unsigned 16-bit integer (`uint16`) to reduce computational requirements.
- Processed MRI scans consisting of 144 slices, each with dimensions of 256 × 256.
- Implemented slice-by-slice analysis across the three different MRI views to help reduce false positives and false negatives.
- Examined voxel matrices and identified computational challenges associated with processing large MRI volumes.
- Explored the possibility of using C++ for more efficient processing of binary MRI data.

## Tools and Technologies
- Python
- MRI image processing libraries
- Binary image representation
- C++

## Next Week
- Implement the binary MRI processing workflow in C++.
- Test the implementation on individual MRI scans before extending it to multiple scans.
- Compare the accuracy of Python and C++ implementations.
- Improve the segmentation process to identify tumor regions more effectively.
- Explore ways to integrate Python and C++ implementations.

